## RDP Command Reference — Windows / AD Targets

### xfreerdp (preferred for modern Windows)

```bash
# Basic connection with password
xfreerdp /v:$IP /u:username /p:'password'

# With domain (AD environment — always specify domain)
xfreerdp /v:$IP /u:username /p:'password' /d:corp.local

# Ignore certificate warning (almost always needed on lab boxes)
xfreerdp /v:$IP /u:username /p:'password' /d:corp.local /cert:ignore

# Pass the Hash (no plaintext password needed)
xfreerdp /v:$IP /u:administrator /pth:NTLMhash /cert:ignore

# Non-standard port
xfreerdp /v:$IP:3390 /u:username /p:'password' /cert:ignore

# Full quality — useful flags for a clean session
xfreerdp /v:$IP /u:username /p:'password' /d:corp.local /cert:ignore \
  +clipboard /dynamic-resolution /drive:share,/tmp

# Drive sharing — mounts your Kali /tmp as a share inside the RDP session
# Useful for transferring tools without a separate file transfer step
xfreerdp /v:$IP /u:username /p:'password' /cert:ignore /drive:kali,/home/kali/tools

# If connection drops or is unstable
xfreerdp /v:$IP /u:username /p:'password' /cert:ignore /timeout:80000

# Older protocol fallback (sometimes needed for older Server 2008/2012 boxes)
xfreerdp /v:$IP /u:username /p:'password' /cert:ignore /sec:rdp

# If you get "credssp" or NLA errors — cycle through these
xfreerdp /v:$IP /u:username /p:'password' /cert:ignore /sec:tls
xfreerdp /v:$IP /u:username /p:'password' /cert:ignore /sec:nla
xfreerdp /v:$IP /u:username /p:'password' /cert:ignore /sec:rdp
```

> **xfreerdp troubleshooting:** The single most common reason it fails is a missing `/cert:ignore` flag. The second is NLA enforcement — try cycling through `/sec:rdp`, `/sec:tls`, and `/sec:nla` until one works.

---

### rdesktop (older tool — inconsistent on modern Windows)

```bash
# Basic
rdesktop $IP

# With credentials
rdesktop -u username -p 'password' $IP

# With domain
rdesktop -u username -p 'password' -d corp.local $IP

# Specify geometry
rdesktop -u username -p 'password' -g 1280x800 $IP

# Full screen
rdesktop -u username -p 'password' -f $IP

# Older security protocol (helps with Windows Server 2003/2008)
rdesktop -u username -p 'password' -r sound:off $IP
```

> **Why rdesktop often fails on modern targets:** It doesn't support NLA properly, which is enabled by default on Windows 10, Server 2016 and later. If you get an immediate disconnect, that's almost certainly why. Use xfreerdp instead for anything modern.

